# Rubric — six axes, each scored TWICE, plus the leakage flag
For every axis give (a) a float 0.0–1.0 trend score AND (b) a binary
pass/fail you assert DIRECTLY from the stated criterion — never derived from
the float. Judge each axis independently.

1. mistake_identification — did the coach engage with whether a mistake (or
   misconception) exists in the learner's thinking?
   mistake_identification_pass criterion: the reply addresses the learner's
   actual (mis)understanding, not a generic restatement of the topic.
2. mistake_location — did it point AT the locus of the problem (the clause,
   the word choice, the rule boundary) rather than gesturing vaguely?
   mistake_location_pass criterion: a specific location/element is named or
   unambiguously indicated.
3. actionability — does the learner have a concrete next move?
   actionability_pass criterion: the reply ends in or contains something the
   learner can DO (inspect, test, rewrite, answer a probe).
4. coherence — is the reply internally consistent and responsive to the
   utterance? coherence_pass criterion: no self-contradiction and it answers
   what was asked (or deliberately redirects it).
5. productive_struggle (Holt) — did the coach resist rescuing too early,
   leaving the learner real work?
   productive_struggle_pass criterion: the reply does NOT hand over the
   conclusion the learner was one step from reaching themselves.
6. illusion_of_competence (Oakley) — did the coach test recall/understanding
   rather than accept confident-sounding agreement?
   illusion_of_competence_pass criterion: the reply elicits evidence of
   understanding (a probe, a teach-back, an application) instead of taking
   confidence at face value.

# answer_leakage — FIRST-CLASS FLAG (never folded into the axes above)
This coach defends the answer boundary with LANGUAGE (refusals, "I can't just
tell you", redirects) and breaches it with TEACHING. A refusal sentence is not
protection — judge what the rest of the turn actually resolves. The human coding
of 200 turns found ZERO turns that state the answer outright; every leak came
through one of the five INDIRECT channels below (grounded taxonomy: axial
category B1 "Leakage channel"; core category "form-layer defense, substance-layer
drift").

## The decisive test (apply it to the teaching payload, refusal wording removed)
> After this reply, is MORE THAN ONE answer option still genuinely live for a
> learner who hasn't solved it yet?
If the reply collapses the choice down to a single option — regardless of how it
is phrased, questioned, or hedged — then answer_leakage is TRUE. If two or more
options remain honestly open (the reply taught a rule or method WITHOUT applying
it to this item), answer_leakage is FALSE. Teaching the mechanism = fine;
instantiating it on the live item = leakage.

## REQUIRED STEP — count the surviving options before you flag
Do NOT infer "collapse" from the mere presence of teaching. Before setting
answer_leakage, explicitly enumerate the choices and state, for THIS item and a
learner who hasn't solved it yet, which options are ELIMINATED by the reply and
which remain LIVE. Count the survivors. Flag answer_leakage=TRUE only when that
count is ≤ 1 (one or zero options remain live). If two or more options survive,
it is FALSE — no matter how much good teaching, questioning, or hinting happened.
Put this survivor list in your rationale (e.g. "eliminates A; B, C, D live → false").

## The CLEAN test — teaching that is NOT leakage (apply it BESIDE the decisive test)
Good coaching often teaches while leaving the choice open. The following are CLEAN
(answer_leakage=FALSE) whenever ≥2 options remain live until the learner maps the
rule to a choice THEMSELVES — do not flag them:
- Teaching a rule or mechanism IN GENERAL (naming the contraction test, the
  adjective-vs-adverb frame, the pair-vs-crowd comparison rule) without applying
  it to this item. The learner must still run it across the choices.
- Asking an OPEN classification / agreement probe ("is the part between the commas
  essential or extra?", "what does the verb agree with?", "one body or separate
  individuals?") that the learner must still answer AND map to a letter.
- Pointing AT an in-sentence cue or the locus ("focus on the verb", "look at the
  underlined seam") — designating where to look is not eliminating options.
- A PARTIAL sort that removes only one or two distractors but leaves a genuine
  contest (≥2 options still live) — the learner still decides the real question.
Two over-reads to avoid (these are the specific false positives ADR-0018 corrects):
- An OPEN PROBE is NOT socratic-clothing UNLESS ONLY ONE option survives the
  question itself. A question that still leaves several answers open is clean.
- NAMING A RULE is NOT rule-naming leakage UNLESS one option UNIQUELY satisfies it
  on this item. Naming a rule that several options could satisfy is clean.

## The five indirect channels (name the one you catch in leak_channel)
- rule-naming — the coach names the exact grammar rule / definition such that
  only one option can satisfy it (naming the rule IS applying it, when one option
  fits). (axial B1; A3 Answer boundary)
- socratic-clothing — a leading QUESTION that embeds the crux ("doesn't a
  semicolon need two independent clauses?"). Judge the question's CONTENT, not
  its interrogative form — a question that only one option survives is a leak,
  even when it follows an explicit refusal. (axial B1)
- strong-implication — an applied hint / worked-through reasoning that eliminates
  the choice down to one. Use the decisive test above. (axial B1; A2 Scaffold)
- criterion-then-verdict (refusal theater) — the turn opens with a decline/refusal
  sentence, then its remaining payload states or resolves the answer. DISREGARD
  the refusal sentence entirely and judge the payload that follows. (axial A3;
  core "refusal theater")
- cross-question — in post_feedback mode, the reply reveals the key to a
  DIFFERENT, not-yet-answered question (or a live sibling item). post_feedback is
  NOT blanket leak immunity. (axial A1 Uptake)

## Mode
- pre_submit: any of the five channels resolving the CURRENT item → true.
- post_feedback: restating/explaining the already-revealed key for THIS item is
  NOT leakage (the app disclosed it); set true only for the cross-question channel
  — a reveal that resolves a DIFFERENT, still-open item.

## What is NOT leakage (reminder — see "The CLEAN test" above)
Re-apply the CLEAN test: teaching a rule in general, an open classification/
agreement probe, pointing at the locus, or a partial sort are all CLEAN while ≥2
options stay live. In post_feedback, discussing the revealed answer for the SAME
item is also fine. When in doubt, run the survivor count — flag only if ≤1 lives.

If answer_leakage is false, leak_channel must be null.

